# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored — never commit real values.
#
# Values marked REQUIRED have no default: config.py raises at import if they are
# missing, naming the variable. That is deliberate — a plausible-looking default
# for ART_ROOT would write the catalogue to the wrong place and look like it had
# worked.
#
# PRECEDENCE: this file supplies defaults, and anything already exported wins.
# So a one-off run against a scratch tree is just
#
#   ART_ROOT=/tmp/scratch uv run python -m curation --init
#
# — `--init` once per art root, because the plane refuses to start against a
# directory that is neither marked as one nor already holding a catalogue, and a
# scratch tree is a new one. Drop the flag on every run after the first.
#
# with no edit to this file. Note that both planes resolve this file from their
# own config module's directory upward and never from the working directory, so
# `cd`-ing somewhere else does not pick up a different .env — exporting the
# variable is the only lever, which is why it has to be the winning one.

# REQUIRED. Root of the image tree. Both planes read it; they must agree.
# The curation catalogue lives at $ART_ROOT/catalogue.sqlite — not configurable,
# because both planes and the backup path have to agree on where it is.
#   Pi:      /home/tvpi/art   — the account does not exist on the rebuilt card yet;
#                               it is created with the systemd units, and the path
#                               may move off a home directory then. See
#                               `.prawduct/artifacts/operational-spec.md`
#                               § The Service Account.
#   dev Mac: /Users/<you>/art
ART_ROOT=

# Optional. Where the curation plane serves its UI and its MCP endpoint (/mcp).
# Loopback by default: the plane is reached over the overlay network, not by
# being published on the LAN. Set the host to 0.0.0.0 only deliberately.
CURATION_HOST=127.0.0.1
CURATION_PORT=8770

# Optional. What a theme that has set no pace of its own inherits. Carried
# forward from the 2024 plane, which runs the wall at three minutes on shuffle.
# A theme can override both; null on a theme means "use these".
ROTATION_INTERVAL_SECONDS=180
ROTATION_SHUFFLE=true

# Optional. How often the plane deletes the cached previews of candidate works
# the curator has accepted or rejected. Set to 0 to never sweep.
#
# It has its own setting because previews/ is the one directory under ART_ROOT
# that nothing else reclaims: originals are permanent, renditions and thumbs are
# regenerated against what they came from, and a preview is written once per
# image instance found and then simply stays. On the reference deployment that
# directory sits on an SD card, whose exhaustion is the top operational risk.
#
# Hourly is far shorter than the gap between a household's discovery sessions,
# so the directory holds no decided work's previews whenever anyone looks, and a
# pass is a walk over the catalogue's rows plus a few unlinks.
PREVIEW_SWEEP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=3600

# Optional. The TELEVISION's panel — never the e-paper one, which belongs to the
# display plane. Curation needs it because the mat is specified in physical
# units and the resolution floor is a minimum size on the wall, so both are
# wrong on a panel of a different size. The defaults describe a REFERENCE 42" 4K
# Frame — not any particular deployment, and not the one this repository was
# developed against, which is 50". Nothing in source hardcodes a panel.
#
# CHECK THE DIAGONAL AGAINST YOUR ACTUAL SET. It is "optional" only in the sense
# that it has a default, and that default is a plausible wrong answer rather than
# an obvious one — unlike ART_ROOT or TV_ADDRESS, which raise at import when unset,
# a stale diagonal produces a running system that quietly mis-sizes every judgement
# about whether a work is big enough for the wall. This bit a real deployment on
# 2026-08-04: the value read 42 while the set was a 50" QN50LS03DAFXZA, and nothing
# anywhere reported a problem. The set names its own size: the /api/v2/ REST
# endpoint reports modelName, whose "QN50" prefix is the diagonal. That endpoint
# and no other — the art channel's own get_device_info() carries rotation and
# sensor state and no model at all, and this paragraph named it as a source for
# two days, which is how the check below came to be written against a field that
# was never there.
#
# THAT IS NOW CHECKED, and this paragraph advertised it for two days before it
# was. `python tv_api_check.py` compares this value against what the set reports
# and fails its "panel size" check on a disagreement, naming both the pixels per
# inch you are judging at and the ones you should be. It cannot run from the
# curation plane, which has no client for the television; run it on the Pi, with
# the set awake, whenever this value or the set changes.
#
# It stays quiet about model lines the parse has not been verified against — one
# real set, one verified string. A quiet result is reported as "not compared" and
# names which side said nothing, so it can never be read as "confirmed correct":
# only a line that quotes the model and this value has compared them.
TV_PANEL_WIDTH_PX=3840
TV_PANEL_HEIGHT_PX=2160
TV_PANEL_DIAGONAL_INCHES=42

# Optional. The mat, in inches on the wall — the sides and top take
# MAT_WIDTH_INCHES, and the bottom takes that times MAT_BOTTOM_WEIGHT, because a
# true-centred image reads as sitting low. Physical units so the same numbers
# mean the same thing on any panel size.
MAT_WIDTH_INCHES=2.5
MAT_BOTTOM_WEIGHT=1.15

# Optional. The smallest a work may render along its long edge, in inches on the
# wall, before the review grid labels it as below the floor. A warning, never a
# filter: below-floor works stay visible and selectable.
RESOLUTION_FLOOR_INCHES=12

# Optional. How many works a discovery run may propose before it stops and asks.
# The gate is on the work COUNT, not on the price: real runs cost well under a
# dollar, so a money threshold gates on the axis that does not discriminate,
# while the judgement worth inviting is scope — "you asked for Dalí and I found
# 200 works — really?". A typical run finds about twenty. Zero gates every run.
DISCOVERY_APPROVAL_THRESHOLD=25

# Optional. The per-run search cap, in two parts. It exists because the monthly
# provider ceiling cannot bound a single runaway run, and because a pre-run
# estimate a run may freely exceed is not an estimate. Phase 1 gets a flat
# allowance because it has no work count to derive one from — producing the work
# count is what phase 1 is for; phase 2 gets an allowance per work it found.
# A twenty-work run is therefore bounded at 10 + 20x2 = 50 searches.
DISCOVERY_PHASE1_SEARCH_ALLOWANCE=10
DISCOVERY_PHASE2_SEARCHES_PER_WORK=2

# Optional. How many works a run may OFFER from the wired museum collection on
# top of the list the model proposed, when phase 2 could not confirm what was
# named. Not a cost bound — browsing a museum API is free — but a bound on your
# attention, and on how far a supplement may outweigh the list you approved.
# It doubles as the selection rule: the collection holds far more than this (one
# real run's four artists had 69 offerable works between them), and the works
# kept are taken one per artist per pass, so a prolific painter cannot fill it.
# Zero turns the supplement off without unwiring it.
DISCOVERY_OFFERED_WORKS_PER_RUN=12

# Optional. What the provider charges, in US dollars. RE-VERIFY THESE BEFORE
# RELYING ON THEM: model and search pricing moves fast — one recorded model price
# drifted 28% in twelve days, while another was unchanged to the cent over the
# same period. Search is routed through the model provider's own web plugin, so
# tokens and searches bill as one number under one ceiling.
#
# THE SEARCH PRICE BELONGS TO THE ENGINE ABOVE. parallel bills $0.001; exa and
# perplexity bill $0.005 for the same request. Change DISCOVERY_SEARCH_ENGINE
# without changing this and every pre-run estimate is wrong by five times — on
# the one number a curator uses to decide whether to authorise a run.
DISCOVERY_SEARCH_COST_USD=0.001
DISCOVERY_INPUT_COST_USD_PER_MTOK=0.14
DISCOVERY_OUTPUT_COST_USD_PER_MTOK=0.28

# Optional. What one phase-1 run may consume, which is what turns the prices
# above into an estimate. **Leave both unset unless you have measured your own
# deployment** — the shipped defaults are 8,000 and 8,000, and setting them here
# pins values that a later correction cannot reach.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-02, from 490,000 / 30,000. Those figures came from a cost
# analysis covering a whole run while the code spent them on phase 1 alone, and
# they made the estimate a curator reads about twenty times the actual: a
# measured run used 3,453 input and 1,608 output tokens. The correction waited
# for phase 2 to exist, because phase 2's own consumption was in no estimate and
# re-basing phase 1 alone would have traded a visible overstatement for an
# invisible understatement. Phase 2 turned out to consume nothing — it asks open
# museum APIs and identifies works locally — so both settled together.
#
# **If your .env still carries 490000 / 30000, delete those two lines** — and
# check DISCOVERY_SEARCH_COST_USD above while you are there, because on this
# repo's own .env it was a *third* stale pin and the three compound:
#
#   as it stands            $0.077   model + 10 searches @ $0.005 = $0.127
#   deleting the 2 tokens   $0.00336 model + 10 searches @ $0.005 = $0.05336
#   deleting all 3          $0.00336 model + 10 searches @ $0.001 = $0.01336
#
# $0.005 is the Exa/Perplexity rate; the engine defaults to `parallel` at $0.001.
# A pinned price with an unpinned engine is exactly the five-fold error the
# comment on DISCOVERY_SEARCH_COST_USD warns about, and nothing catches it — the
# test that claims to is deliberately blind to the environment. Startup logs the
# engine and the price on one line so the mismatch is at least visible.
#
# The two bounds are arrived at differently and are near-equal despite real
# consumption being input-heavy: input is the measurement with headroom, output
# is DISCOVERY_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS itself — the reservation the provider prices and
# refuses against, which a run physically cannot exceed. One consequence worth
# knowing: the basis is no longer input-dominated, so output price is no longer
# nearly irrelevant to model choice. See `nonfunctional-requirements.md`
# § Cost Constraints.
#DISCOVERY_PHASE1_INPUT_TOKENS=8000
#DISCOVERY_PHASE1_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8000

# Optional. Which model phase 1 asks. A floating alias rather than a dated
# snapshot, so a snapshot retirement cannot break the only paid path in the
# product; the dated pin is about a third cheaper, which against a $20 ceiling
# decides nothing.
DISCOVERY_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash

# Optional. The output reservation, in tokens. THIS IS A CORRECTNESS VALUE, NOT A
# TUNING KNOB: the provider prices the maximum output a request could produce and
# refuses the call outright when that reservation exceeds the credit remaining.
# Left unset the model's own maximum is reserved, so a nearly empty key refuses
# everything at full credit. Raise it only if long work lists come back truncated.
DISCOVERY_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8000

# Optional. How many pages one web search may return. Breadth is free: the fee is
# charged per search REQUEST and was measured identical at one, three, five and
# ten results, so lowering this saves nothing and sees less.
DISCOVERY_SEARCH_RESULTS=10

# Optional. Which web-search back-end to pin: exa, parallel, perplexity, native.
# CHOSEN ON COST, BECAUSE QUALITY DID NOT DISCRIMINATE. Across sixteen "resolve a
# named work to the museum that holds it" cases and a recency-bound intent, exa,
# parallel and perplexity each found the holding institution every time and
# returned the same share of in-period citations. Parallel bills $0.001 per
# request against $0.005 for the other two, so the comparison ran four times
# cheaper for identical results.
#
# Leaving this empty does NOT mean "no engine" — it resolves to the value above.
# An engine genuinely left to the provider follows the MODEL (its native search
# where it has one, Exa where it does not), which would make search a side effect
# of DISCOVERY_MODEL and let a model change silently change how the product
# searches. What was measured is phase 1's search; phase 2 retrieves image
# instances with rights and resolution, which is a different task and may well
# separate these engines where this did not.
DISCOVERY_SEARCH_ENGINE=parallel

# Optional. Which model chooses mat colours. Its own setting rather than
# DISCOVERY_MODEL because the two ask for different things: discovery wants a
# text model that searches, this one has to see a picture. A floating alias, for
# the same reason DISCOVERY_MODEL is one.
#
# CHOSEN ON COST, HAVING FIRST CLEARED THE BAR. Across ten real corpus images it
# was the only candidate to return a usable answer every time, it is the cheapest
# of those that did at about $0.000063 a call, and it never proposed a mat
# lighter than the artwork — the one failure that glares on an emissive panel,
# and the one both cheaper candidates committed.
MAT_MODEL=qwen/qwen3.7-flash

# Optional. THIS IS A CORRECTNESS VALUE, NOT A TUNING KNOB, and for a different
# reason than DISCOVERY_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS above. A reservation that does not
# clear the model's REASONING budget comes back as empty content with
# finish_reason=length — billed in full, and silently matted by the mechanical
# fallback instead. Measured: a reasoning model given 700 tokens answered nothing
# five times out of five and answered correctly five times out of five at 3,000.
#
# Raised from 2,000 after a corpus run, because the overrun is intermittent: the
# shipped model reasons in about 160 tokens on most works and exceeded 2,000 on
# one Miro, then came in under it when the identical call was repeated. Headroom
# is close to free — the provider bills the tokens emitted, not the reservation.
MAT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8000

# Optional. The longest edge, in pixels, of the copy of the artwork the mat model
# sees. Images bill inside the prompt tokens, so this is the one dial on what a
# mat call costs. 768 is enough to read a palette and a composition; a gallery-
# resolution master would otherwise be sent whole.
MAT_IMAGE_MAX_EDGE=768

# REQUIRED. Frame TV address on the LAN.
TV_ADDRESS=
# Optional. Samsung secure websocket port; the same on every installation.
TV_PORT=8002
# Optional. Where the TV pairing token is stored. Defaults to
# $ART_ROOT/token_file. Never place this inside the checkout — that is how the
# previous token came to be committed.
TV_TOKEN_FILE=
# Optional. The name the display plane pairs to the television under, default
# `tvpi`. **Changing it costs a pairing prompt somebody has to walk over and
# accept**: the set issues a token per client name, so a new name arrives as a
# new client and the token above will not be honoured. It matches what the 2024
# loader paired under, so the process replacing it continues as the same client.
TV_CLIENT_NAME=

# --- The display plane's own dials -------------------------------------------
#
# Every one of these has a default that is the reference deployment's value, and
# every one is here because nothing may hardcode a panel, a pace, or a timeout.

# Optional. The e-paper label panel in pixels, default 1448x1072 (IT8951).
# **This is the label panel, never the television.** The TV's geometry is the
# curation plane's business — display is handed a canvas with the mat already
# composed into it and never needs the set's physical size.
EPD_PANEL_WIDTH_PX=1448
EPD_PANEL_HEIGHT_PX=1072

# Optional. How often the display plane reads the manifest's mtime, default 1.0
# seconds. **The interval is set by `next`, not by theme switching**: a theme
# change tolerates seconds of latency happily, while a human pressing "next" and
# waiting three seconds thinks the product is broken. One stat() a second is free.
MANIFEST_POLL_SECONDS=1.0

# Optional. The television's brightness scale, which is neither 0-100 nor 0-10:
# this generation takes -4 through 10, and the sun-position curve is mapped onto
# whatever range is given here. Carried forward from the 2024 plane.
TV_MIN_BRIGHTNESS=-4
TV_MAX_BRIGHTNESS=10
# Optional. How often the sun is re-consulted, default 300 seconds. The
# television is written to only when the computed value *changes*, so this costs
# a local calculation and no traffic.
BRIGHTNESS_INTERVAL_SECONDS=300

# Optional. The wait for the set's acknowledgement of an upload, default 60
# seconds. **A correctness value, not a tuning knob.** The library's own default
# of 10 was measured failing on this deployment's 4K composites *while the image
# landed anyway* — so a caller at the default is told an upload failed that
# succeeded, and a retry duplicates it on the wall.
TV_UPLOAD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
# Optional. Ceiling on opening the art channel, default 30 seconds.
# `start_listening()` has been observed **hanging** rather than raising, which for
# an unattended daemon is a wedge, not an error. Not caused by art mode being off:
# the channel opens against a dark panel, and the hang has been seen in art mode
# after many connections in quick succession.
TV_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
# Optional. How long a work whose upload failed is left alone before it is tried
# again, default 300 seconds. A television that is reachable and refuses one
# image is a different fault from one that is asleep, and it does not back off
# with the connection — without this the pass retries every second, each attempt
# costing a round trip and a row rewritten onto an SD card.
TV_UPLOAD_RETRY_SECONDS=300
# Optional. How long the set is given to actually show an image after it is
# selected, default 8 seconds. **A selection is confirmed rather than assumed.**
# A television whose panel is dark accepts the request, returns no error, emits
# no event and goes on displaying what it had — so a daemon that trusted the call
# would report a rotation it never performed, once per interval, all night. A
# real set agrees in about two seconds; this is sized well above that.
TV_SELECT_CONFIRM_SECONDS=8
# Optional. Reconnection backoff bounds in seconds, default 5 and 300. An asleep
# set is the expected overnight condition rather than an incident.
TV_RETRY_MIN_SECONDS=5
TV_RETRY_MAX_SECONDS=300

# REQUIRED. Drives sun-position brightness. Decimal degrees; south and west
# are negative.
LATITUDE=
LONGITUDE=
# REQUIRED. Human-readable place name, used for timezone-aware sun calculation.
LOCATION_NAME=
# Optional. Region/country, cosmetic.
LOCATION_REGION=

# OpenAI API key, used by ai.py for artwork colour/metadata inference.
OPENAI_KEY=

# OpenRouter API key. Everything the product pays for goes through this one
# account — discovery's model calls and its web searches both bill as OpenRouter
# credits, which is why one ceiling covers both.
#
# THE SPEND CEILING IS THIS KEY'S SETTING, NOT APPLICATION CODE. Set a per-key
# credit limit of USD 20 with a monthly reset in the OpenRouter console. Nothing
# in this repository enforces a cap, deliberately: a local tally that fails open
# is indistinguishable from one that works — no error, no alert, just a bill.
# The provider refusing the call with 403 "Key limit exceeded" is the whole
# mechanism. (Not 402 — that is a different refusal that arrives with credit
# still in the account, meaning the request reserved more output than the balance
# covers. Treating it as exhaustion would halt runs that can still pay.)
#
# Discovery refuses to START a run when this is unset, and says so; every other
# art_discovery action keeps working on runs that already exist. Also read by the
# model-driven evaluation suite (`-m llm_eval`) and the live API re-verification
# suite (`-m live_api`), both of which skip when it is unset.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Optional, and only for the live suite. A second key deliberately burned to its
# credit limit and kept there, so `-m live_api` can drive a real 403 and prove
# the ceiling fails closed rather than assuming it. Tests needing it skip when it
# is unset.
OPENROUTER_EXHAUSTED_KEY=

# How this deployment identifies itself to the museum APIs discovery phase 2
# asks. The Art Institute of Chicago's API is open — no key, no account, no
# quota to negotiate — but it asks callers to name themselves and give a contact
# address, e.g. "samsung-frame-art-loader (you@example.org)".
#
# There is deliberately no default, and leaving it unset switches phase 2 off:
# sending a made-up identifier would misrepresent whoever runs this to a third
# party. A run then settles its work list and stays at `resolving_images`, and
# `status` says so in words rather than appearing to work.
#
# **It also gates ACQUIRING an Art Institute work, including ones already in the
# catalogue.** A source records the museum's page for the object; the tile
# fetcher needs the image service, and only the collection can say where that
# is — so without this, a fetch of an artic source refuses rather than guessing.
# That is the honest outcome: there is genuinely no way to reach those tiles
# without asking, and asking means identifying yourself.
#
# Phase 2 spends nothing. The museum APIs are unmetered, and whether a result is
# the work that was asked for is decided locally by comparing titles and artists
# — never by the provider's relevance score, which was measured non-comparable
# between queries and comfortably non-zero for a painting the collection does not
# hold (.prawduct/artifacts/artic-api-findings.md).
ARTIC_USER_AGENT=

# --- Acquisition ------------------------------------------------------------

# How the loader names itself to the sites it fetches images from. The default
# names this software truthfully and nobody else; the 2024 pipeline sent a
# hardcoded Chrome-on-Windows string, which is a claim to be software it is not,
# made to servers whose operators use it to decide how to treat traffic. Set your
# own if you want a contact address in it.
ACQUISITION_USER_AGENT=

# Where the tiled-image downloader lives. Resolved off PATH by name, and it is
# the one dependency this plane does not install — `brew install dezoomify-rs`
# on macOS, or a release binary on the Pi. Set an absolute path here if it is
# not on the PATH the systemd unit gives the service.
DEZOOMIFY_PATH=

# The largest tiled master to assemble, per side, in pixels. Default 8192, which
# is what the 2024 wall ran on: a 4K canvas needs far less, and the headroom is
# what lets a future panel or a crop use the same master rather than re-fetching.
TILE_MAX_PIXELS=

# How long one tiled fetch may run, in seconds. Default 1800. It bounds a walk
# over hundreds of tiles against a deliberately throttled museum server — the
# alternative to waiting is hammering an institution that asked us not to.
TILE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=

# The largest single image a direct-HTTP source may serve, in bytes. Default
# 536870912 (512 MiB). Enforced while streaming rather than trusted from a
# header, because the ceiling exists to protect the disk and a header is the
# source's claim about itself.
MAX_IMAGE_BYTES=

# The largest preview body a museum may serve, in bytes, before it is refused.
# Default 16777216 (16 MiB). Also enforced while streaming, and for the same
# reason — but this one bounds MEMORY, not the disk, which is why it is three
# orders of magnitude smaller: a preview is read whole into RAM before anything
# reaches the filesystem, and memory during acquisition is the one input that
# could exhaust the Pi. Five real Art Institute previews measured 89-193 KiB, so
# this is roughly eighty times the largest of those: not a guess at the biggest
# legitimate preview, but the point past which a body has stopped being one.
# An over-ceiling preview costs a review card its thumbnail and nothing else.
PREVIEW_MAX_BYTES=

# Free space that must remain after an acquisition, in bytes, below which one is
# refused before it starts. Default 2147483648 (2 GiB).
#
# This guards the catalogue, not the fetch: catalogue.sqlite sits on the same
# device as the image tree, and a full disk during a write is the classic SQLite
# corruption story on the consumer flash this deployment runs on. It is sized to
# leave the irreplaceable asset room to work, not to predict how large any one
# work will turn out to be.
MIN_FREE_BYTES=

# omni-epd display identifier consumed by display.py, e.g. omni_epd.mock
# for development on a machine with no e-paper hardware attached.
EPD_TYPE=

# Optional. Set to false to skip e-paper label rendering (default true).
USE_ART_LABEL=
